{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In the beginning.</b> Rabbi Yitzchak said: It was not necessary to begin the Torah except from ״הַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה לָכֶם״ — that is the first commandment Israel was commanded. And what is the reason it opened with בְּרֵאשִׁית? Because of ״כֹּחַ מַעֲשָׂיו הִגִּיד לְעַמּוֹ לָתֵת לָהֶם נַחֲלַת גּוֹיִם״ (Psalms 111). For if the nations of the world say to Israel: Bandits, you conquered the lands of the seven nations — they say to them: The whole earth belongs to the Holy One, blessed be He; He created it and gave it to whomever was upright in His eyes; by His will He gave it to them, and by His will He took it from them and gave it to us." "<b>In the beginning of.</b> This verse says nothing but — interpret me! As our Rabbis interpreted it: for the Torah, which is called רֵאשִׁית דַּרְכּוֹ (Proverbs 8), and for Israel, who are called רֵאשִׁית תְּבוּאָתוֹ (Jeremiah 2). And if you come to explain it by its plain sense — explain it thus: בְּרֵאשִׁית בְּרִיאַת שָׁמַיִם וָאָרֶץ, וְהָאָרֶץ הָיְתָה תֹהוּ וָבֹהוּ וְחֹשֶׁךְ, וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים יְהִי אוֹר. And the verse did not come to indicate the order of creation, to say that these came first — for if it came to indicate thus, it should have written ״בָּרִאשׁוֹנָה בָּרָא אֶת הַשָּׁמַיִם״ etc. For you have no רֵאשִׁית in Scripture that is not attached to the word after it — as ״בְּרֵאשִׁית מַמְלֶכֶת יְהוֹיָקִים״ (Jeremiah 27), ״רֵאשִׁית מַמְלַכְתּוֹ״ (Genesis 10), ״רֵאשִׁית דְּגָנְךָ״ (Deuteronomy 18:4) — so too here you say: בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים etc., like בְּרֵאשִׁית בְּרֹא. And similar to it: ״תְּחִלַּת דִּבֶּר ה׳ בְּהוֹשֵׁעַ״ (Hosea 1) — that is, the beginning of the Holy One, blessed be He, speaking with Hosea: וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֶל הוֹשֵׁעַ etc. And if you say it came to indicate that these were created first, and its meaning is בְּרֵאשִׁית הַכֹּל בָּרָא אֵלּוּ — yet there are verses that abbreviate their language and omit one word, as ״כִּי לֹא סָגַר דַּלְתֵי בִטְנִי״ (Job 3) without specifying who closed; and as ״יִשָּׂא אֶת חֵיל דַּמֶּשֶׂק״ (Isaiah 8) without specifying who carried it; and as ״אִם יַחֲרוֹשׁ בַּבָּקָרִים״ (Amos 6) without specifying whether a man plows with oxen; and as ״מַגִּיד מֵרֵאשִׁית אַחֲרִית״ (Isaiah 46) without specifying מַגִּיד מֵרֵאשִׁית דָּבָר אַחֲרִית דָּבָר — if so, be astonished at yourself, for behold, the waters came first, since it is written ״וְרוּחַ אֱלֹהִים מְרַחֶפֶת עַל פְּנֵי הַמָּיִם״, and the verse has not yet revealed when the creation of the waters was. From this you learn that the waters preceded the earth. And furthermore: the heavens were created from fire and water — perforce, the verse did not teach the order of what preceded and what followed at all." "<b>Elohim created.</b> And it was not said ״בָּרָא ה׳״ — because at first it arose in thought to create it with the attribute of justice. He saw that the world could not endure, so He preceded the attribute of mercy and partnered it with the attribute of justice. And that is what is written: ״בְּיוֹם עֲשׂוֹת ה׳ אֱלֹהִים אֶרֶץ וְשָׁמָיִם״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:43:05.893171655Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}